{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "def find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    DEFAULT_TOLERANCE = 1e-6\n    DEFAULT_MAX_ITERATIONS = 100\n\n    def poly_func(val: float) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Helper to evaluate the polynomial with coefficients xs.\"\"\"\n        return poly(xs, val)\n\n    def _bisection_method(\n        func: callable,\n        a: float,\n        b: float,\n        tolerance: float,\n        max_iterations: int\n    ) -> float:\n        \"\"\"\n        Implements the bisection method to find a root of func in [a, b].\n        Assumes func(a) and func(b) have opposite signs or a root is very close to an endpoint.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Per instruction, no explicit error checks for initial signs or non-convergence.\n        # It is assumed that conditions for bisection will generally lead to a solution.\n        \n        for _ in range(max_iterations):\n            c = (a + b) / 2\n            if abs(func(c)) < tolerance:\n                return c\n            \n            # Use `abs` for comparison if one of the values might be extremely close to zero,\n            # which could make product comparison problematic (e.g., `1e-20 * -5 < 0` is true, but `1e-20 * 0 == 0` is not).\n            # A direct sign check is more robust for numerical stability.\n            sign_a = 1 if func(a) > tolerance else (-1 if func(a) < -tolerance else 0)\n            sign_c = 1 if func(c) > tolerance else (-1 if func(c) < -tolerance else 0)\n\n            if sign_a == 0: # a is effectively a root\n                return a\n            if sign_c == 0: # c is effectively a root (already caught by abs(func(c)) < tolerance, but redundant for safety)\n                return c\n            \n            if sign_a * sign_c < 0:\n                b = c\n            else:\n                a = c\n        return c # Return best approximation if max_iterations reached.\n\n    def _find_initial_interval(func: callable) -> tuple[float, float]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Heuristically finds an initial interval [a, b] for root finding.\n        For odd-degree polynomials, a solution is guaranteed to exist.\n        \"\"\"\n        search_range = 100.0\n        step = 0.1 # Using a smaller step for finer initial search.\n        \n        # Check if 0.0 is a root or very close.\n        if abs(func(0.0)) < DEFAULT_TOLERANCE:\n            return -0.1, 0.1\n\n        # Generate points outwards from zero, including positive and negative sides.\n        points_to_check = [0.0]\n        for i in range(1, int(search_range / step) + 1):\n            points_to_check.append(i * step)\n            points_to_check.append(-i * step)\n        \n        # Sort and remove duplicates for clear adjacent checking.\n        points_to_check = sorted(list(set(points_to_check)))\n\n        # Evaluate all points.\n        # Using a tuple (point, value, sign) for robustness.\n        evaluated_data = []\n        for p in points_to_check:\n            val = func(p)\n            if abs(val) < DEFAULT_TOLERANCE: # Found a point that is effectively a root\n                return p - 0.1, p + 0.1 # Return a small interval around it\n            evaluated_data.append((p, val))\n\n        # Search for a sign change between adjacent points.\n        for i in range(len(evaluated_data) - 1):\n            p1, v1 = evaluated_data[i]\n            p2, v2 = evaluated_data[i+1]\n            if v1 * v2 < 0: # Found an interval with a sign change\n                return p1, p2\n        \n        # Fallback: For odd-degree polynomials, an interval spanning from a very large negative\n        # to a very large positive value is guaranteed to contain a root.\n        very_large_value = 1e6 \n        return -very_large_value, very_large_value\n\n    # Execute the search for the zero point.\n    a, b = _find_initial_interval(poly_func)\n    return _bisection_method(poly_func, a, b, DEFAULT_TOLERANCE, DEFAULT_MAX_ITERATIONS)"}
